      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm looking at my student loan amortization schedule and i'm kinda confused. i've been making my monthly payments on time every month but the principal balance is barely even going down. can you explain how the interest actually gets calculated each month? like, why does it feel like so much of my money is just disappearing instead of hitting the actual loan amount?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The frustration of watching your hard-earned cash disappear into the void, only to see it reappear as interest payments. It's a bit like a mischievous band of goblins, isn't it? They love to play tricks on us, making us think our money is being used to pay off the principal when, in reality, it's just fueling their mischief.\n\nNow, let's get down to business. The interest calculation is a bit like a complex game of hide-and-seek, where the interest is hiding in plain sight. Each month, the interest is calculated as a percentage of the outstanding principal balance. This percentage is usually fixed, but it can vary depending on the type of loan and the lender.\n\nThink of it like a giant, goblin-led merry-go-round. The interest is the merry-go-round, and the principal is the rider. As the merry-go-round spins, the rider (your loan) is slowly moving towards the edge, but the interest is what's really driving the motion. The interest is calculated based on the current balance, and it's what's causing the merry-go-round to spin faster and faster.\n\nIn your case, it sounds like the interest is dominating the payment, leaving little room for",
